Overview
Across this bounded case sample, emerging-technology deployment depended on both newly configured digital capabilities and established organizational resources. The analysis excludes the organizational-culture and leadership-adoption category. The remaining dependencies are ranked within each perspective by their summed evidence_count. These counts measure coded evidence density, not prevalence or effect size, and the findings should not be generalized to the construction sector as a whole.
Digitally reconfigured dependencies
1. Interoperable construction data-and-compute backbone (44). Heritage digitization encountered fragmented 3D formats, precision–rendering trade-offs and rural point-cloud transmission failures. Accident-analysis applications required cleaned textual records, maintained domain dictionaries and sufficient training samples, while bid-generation systems were limited by PDF-only processing, missing OCR and small historical repositories. IoT cases additionally reported inaccurate occupancy sensing. Deployment is therefore conditional on common data standards, reliable networks, scalable computing, representative datasets and calibrated sensors; otherwise, digital codification preserves incomplete or erroneous representations of practice (c001;c008;c009;c017).
2. Experiential safety-training design and validation capability (41). AR and VR reconstructed excavation, bridge and confined-space hazards through situated interaction and consequence feedback. Nevertheless, some systems delivered only explicit knowledge, created cognitive overload or assessed retention through short-term tests rather than workplace performance. Significant forgetting also occurred after several weeks. These cases condition deployment on scenario-based pedagogy, spaced rehearsal, realistic feedback and longitudinal behavioural assessment; immersive hardware alone does not demonstrate retention of hazard-recognition or response judgement (c002;c004;c005;c010;c011).
3. Site-calibrated sensing and simulation infrastructure (17). The sampled projects covered congested transport corridors, waterside works, desert conditions, confined spaces and remote heritage locations. Weather, unscheduled classroom occupancy, open windows and inaccurate people-counting disrupted predicted conditions. Wearable measurements likewise required researchers to observe work and interpret video-recorded loads. Digital models and IoT systems must therefore incorporate local baselines, live environmental data and expert-supported recalibration. Without contextual calibration, retained models may misrepresent the conditions under which construction judgement is exercised (c001;c005;c006;c017).
4. Ergonomic and cognitively adaptive digital interfaces (10). Older trainees experienced unfamiliarity with VR controllers, some participants withdrew from immersive training, and cybersickness remained a documented risk. Mixed learning preferences, limited working memory and expertise-reversal effects also altered how users processed digital instruction. Deployment consequently requires usability testing, adjustable instructional complexity, accessible controls and non-immersive alternatives. Otherwise, the technology can impose cognitive or physiological burdens that restrict participation and knowledge absorption (c003;c005;c006;c010;c011).
Persistently established dependencies
1. Construction regulatory, procurement and market alignment (27). Safety practices were anchored in Australian, Chinese and Peruvian regulations, while bid preparation remained governed by client-specific requirements, deadlines and quality–cost selection criteria. Heritage applications were also conditioned by commercialization pressures, and megaproject operations faced regulatory change and logistical instability. Technology did not remove these external architectures; deployment remains conditional on jurisdictional traceability, procurement compatibility and responsiveness to market and logistical change (c001;c002;c009;c014;c016).
2. Protected construction-domain expertise and staffing capacity (20). Bridge-hazard identification required safety-manager interviews and expert prioritization; VR production demanded time-intensive content development. Generative bid preparation still relied on contextual expert review, while narrative capture required facilitation, transcription and curation. Limited familiarity with BIM and occupational-safety applications further constrained implementation. Organizations therefore require protected staff time and personnel who can translate between technical systems and situated construction knowledge (c005;c009;c012;c016).
3. Project knowledge-continuity and information-governance architecture (19). Staff turnover, retirement and temporary project teams dispersed experience across the cases. Centralized repositories improved retention, but fragmented documentation, third-party API confidentiality, hallucination, consent and access restrictions remained. Deployment is conditional on succession arrangements, provenance controls, centralized stewardship, anonymization and governed retrieval; otherwise, digitization relocates rather than resolves knowledge-loss and security risks (c007;c009;c012;c016).
4. Lifecycle capital and physical equipment provision (8). High-definition modeling, sensing equipment, active assistive suits, video production and KM-system maintenance imposed continuing costs. Lower-cost AR delivery or passive suits offered case-specific compromises but did not eliminate financing requirements. Adoption therefore depends on lifecycle budgets, maintenance capacity and sustained access to devices rather than one-off technology acquisition (c001;c006;c007;c010;c012).
Together, digitally reconfigured dependencies make experience capturable, computable and rehearsable, while persistent dependencies provide regulatory legitimacy, expert interpretation, governance and material continuity. Their interaction converts situated individual experience into trustworthy and reusable organizational knowledge; weakness in either perspective leaves retention technically unreliable or operationally unsustainable.